# Exact cell-union localization for the Target A scaffold

This note records a geometric consequence of the exact three-site/four-site
conflict classification. It is a localization result, not a closure of Erdős
Problem #99.

Let \(\Lambda\) be the unit triangular lattice and let \(X\) be the blocker shore
of a compatible-collar scaffold. For \(x\in X\), let

\[
N_\Lambda(x)=\{v\in\Lambda:|x-v|<1\},\qquad
C_x=\operatorname{conv}N_\Lambda(x).
\]

The scaffold classification gives three vertices of an elementary unit triangle
or four vertices of a unit lozenge, and all of those vertices are at strict distance
less than one from \(x\). This immediately implies a stronger geometric statement
than the incidence definition alone suggests.

## Cell-cover lemma

For every \(x\in X\),

\[
\boxed{C_x\subset B(x,1).}
\tag{1}
\]

Indeed, if \(z=\sum_v\lambda_vv\in C_x\), where \(\lambda_v\ge0\) and
\(\sum_v\lambda_v=1\), then

\[
|z-x|\le\sum_v\lambda_v|v-x|<\sum_v\lambda_v=1.
\]

The strict inequality survives because the cell has finitely many vertices and
every vertex clearance is strict. This proof applies to both a monomer triangle and
a dimer lozenge; no regularity of the actual blocker position beyond the classified
vertex inequalities is used.

## Exact lattice-hole identity

The reverse inclusion \(N_\Lambda(x)\subseteq\Lambda\cap C_x\) is tautological. The
cell-cover lemma gives the other inclusion: every lattice site \(v\in C_x\) obeys
\(|v-x|<1\). Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\Lambda\cap C_x=N_\Lambda(x).
}
\tag{2}
\]

For a finite cell family define its geometric union

\[
\mathcal U_X=\bigcup_{x\in X}C_x.
\]

Taking unions in (2) gives

\[
\boxed{
\Lambda\cap\mathcal U_X=N_\Lambda(X).
}
\tag{3}
\]

Hence, for the inner lattice shore \(Y_{\rm in}\),

\[
\boxed{
Y_{\rm in}\setminus N_\Lambda(X)
=Y_{\rm in}\setminus\mathcal U_X.
}
\tag{4}
\]

The right side is the set of lattice sites lying in geometric gaps of the cell
union. Every such site is conflict-free with the entire blocker shore. It is an
independent right-side set, so it gives the immediate Hall certificate

\[
\boxed{
h(G)\ge
\left|Y_{\rm in}\setminus\mathcal U_X\right|.
}
\tag{5}
\]

This identifies the previously abstract free-sink count \(z\) with a spatial
lattice-hole count. In a hypothetical survivor, \(h(G)\le j-1\), so at most \(j-1\)
inner lattice sites can lie outside the cell union:

\[
\boxed{
\left|Y_{\rm in}\setminus\mathcal U_X\right|\le j-1.
}
\tag{6}
\]

This is useful because it separates the two possible sources of the remaining Hall
deficit. If (6) fails, a direct free-sink certificate closes the scaffold. If (6)
holds, every additional deficit must come from a nontrivial deletion/cycle or
boundary-routing certificate inside a lattice-hole-sparse cell union.

## What the lemma does not prove

The lattice-hole identity is intentionally discrete. A geometrically long, narrow
gap can contain no lattice vertex in its interior, so a bound on the number of free
lattice sites does not by itself bound gap area, inradius, or the number of
nonlattice source replacement points. It also does not force a cell union with few
lattice holes to be a single monomer or dimer phase. The all-\(+\) monomer and
lozenge/dimer crystalline regressions remain exact zero-Hall-deficiency models once
the right shore is truncated to the cell neighborhood.

Thus the exact Target A endpoint is unchanged:

\[
\dim\ker S\ge\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int},
\]

or equivalently \(h(G)\ge j\). The new certificate tells us precisely where a
failure of the easy free-sink route must live: in a lattice-hole-sparse geometric
cell union with nontrivial Hall routing.

The executable exact margins for the unit-triangle and unit-lozenge cases are in
`research/cell_union_certificate.py`, with tests in
`research/test_cell_union_certificate.py`.
